course: biology
lesson: 145
title: Shrek's Donkey: real donkeys and mules
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: In Shrek, Donkey never stops talking, digs his hooves in when he is scared, begs for waffles and carries Shrek and Fiona on his back.
explain: Real donkeys are mostly quiet, but their bray is so loud it carries about three kilometres, because wild donkeys live spread out across dry, rocky lands and need to keep in touch.
explain: The famous «stubbornness» is really caution: on a rocky desert path, running blindly could mean a fall, so a donkey stops and thinks where a horse would bolt.
explain: Picture a donkey sanctuary in a rainy country: donkeys crowded in a shed because their desert coats are not waterproof, two best friends grooming each other, and one rolling happily in its favourite dusty patch.
fact: Real donkeys are fairly quiet, but a bray can be heard about three kilometres away; the «hee» is breathed in and the «haw» breathed out.
fact: Donkeys are not really stubborn: when something seems dangerous they stop to check instead of running, which kept wild donkeys safe on rocky ground.
fact: Donkeys' very long ears catch far-off calls and give off heat in hot deserts, and each ear can turn on its own.
fact: Donkey coats are not waterproof like horses' coats, so donkeys need shelter from the rain.
fact: Donkeys form strong friendships and can become very upset if their friend is taken away.
fact: Some farmers keep donkeys to guard sheep and goats, because donkeys dislike dogs and foxes and may chase or kick them away, though not every donkey does.
fact: Donkeys often live 30 to 40 years and some reach 50, longer than most horses.
fact: A mule has a donkey father and a horse mother; it is strong like a horse and sure-footed like a donkey, and mules carry loads down the Grand Canyon.
fact: Donkeys need mostly straw and tough grass; sweet, rich food makes them fat and can make their hooves sore.
fact: Donkeys love to roll in dust, which scratches itches, cleans the coat and keeps off flies.
fact: Donkeys were tamed in north-east Africa about 7,000 years ago, and tens of millions still carry water, crops and firewood today.
exercise: Are real donkeys noisy all the time like Shrek's Donkey? | No, they are mostly quiet, but their bray is very loud | Yes, they bray all day long without a break, even when nobody is there | No, donkeys cannot make any sound | Yes, but only when they are asleep
exercise: How far away can a donkey's bray be heard? | About three kilometres | Only about three metres | Only about thirty metres | About three hundred kilometres
exercise: Why does a donkey stop and refuse to move? | It is checking for danger instead of running | It is lazy and just wants to stand still and rest for a while | It has forgotten how to walk | It is angry with its owner
exercise: Why did stopping to check keep wild donkeys safe? | Running blindly on rocky ground could mean a fall | It let them hide behind bushes | It helped them catch food | It made them look bigger and scarier to the lions hunting them
exercise: What do a donkey's long ears do? | Hear far-off calls and give off heat | Store water for dry days | Help it balance when running | Keep the sun out of its eyes
exercise: Why does a donkey need a shed in rainy weather? | Its coat is not waterproof like a horse's | It is afraid of the sound of rain falling on the roof above it | Rain makes its ears fall off | It cannot see when it rains
exercise: What happens if a donkey's friend is taken away? | It can become very upset | It does not notice at all | It is much happier on its own | It finds a new friend at once
exercise: Why do some farmers keep a donkey with their sheep? | Donkeys may chase or kick away foxes and dogs | Donkeys round up the sheep like dogs | Donkeys keep the sheep warm at night | Donkeys eat the poisonous weeds in the field that would hurt the sheep
exercise: How long do donkeys often live? | About 30 to 40 years | Only about 3 to 4 years | About 10 years at most | About 100 years or more
exercise: What are a mule's parents? | A donkey father and a horse mother | A horse father and a zebra mother | Two donkeys of different colours | A donkey mother and a goat father
exercise: Why are mules used on steep canyon paths? | They are strong and sure-footed | They are the fastest runners of all | They can climb trees if they slip | They never need any water or food
exercise: Is it good to give a donkey waffles and sweets? | No, rich food makes it fat and can make its hooves sore | Yes, sweet things like waffles give donkeys strong and healthy teeth | Yes, waffles are their natural food | No, donkeys only eat meat
exercise: What should most of a donkey's food be? | Straw and tough grass | Fresh bread and sweet cake | Carrots and apples only | Oats and sugar every day
exercise: Why does a donkey roll in a patch of dust? | To scratch itches, clean its coat and keep off flies | Because it has fallen over | To cool down in mud | To hide from other donkeys by rolling its coat the colour of dust
exercise: Where and when were donkeys first tamed? | In north-east Africa about 7,000 years ago | In Australia about 200 years ago, when the first farmers arrived there | In Europe about 100 years ago | In America about 70,000 years ago
exercise: How does a donkey make its «hee-haw»? | Breathing in for «hee» and out for «haw» | Shouting the same sound twice | Clicking its teeth and humming | Breathing out for both sounds
test: «5 donkeys at the sanctuary, how many ears to scratch» | 10 | 5 | 20 | 15
test: «donkeys are stubborn as hell, mine just stopped dead on the path» What is most likely? | It saw something that looked dangerous and stopped to check | It was being lazy on purpose because it did not want to carry the load | It hates its owner | It forgot where it was going
test: In a cartoon a donkey eats a whole cake and is fine. What is real? | Rich, sweet food is bad for donkeys and can make their hooves sore | Donkeys can eat any food at all, cake or sweets, without any harm | Cake is good for donkey teeth | Donkeys eat only cake at parties
test: What would happen if a donkey were left out in cold rain for days with no shelter? | It would get soaked and cold, because its coat is not waterproof | Nothing, its thick coat keeps all the rain out just like a raincoat | It would grow a waterproof coat in a day | It would swim away to find shelter
test: «Donkey's still alive when Shrek's kids are born, cartoon nonsense, donkeys die young» What is right? | Real donkeys often live 30 to 40 years, longer than most horses | Yes, donkeys usually live only about 5 years, much less than a horse | Yes, donkeys die before they are 10 | No, donkeys live for 200 years
test: «a mule's just a donkey with a horse's coat» What is right? | A mule has a donkey father and a horse mother | Yes, it is a donkey that grew a horse's coat | No, it is a kind of small horse | No, it is a donkey crossed with a zebra
test: A farmer's sheep stopped going missing after she bought a donkey. What is the likely reason? | The donkey chased off foxes and dogs | The donkey ate all the foxes it could catch | The donkey made the sheep run faster than foxes | The sheep stayed inside the donkey's shed
test: «that donkey's crying cos its mate went to the vet» Is that likely? | Yes, donkeys bond strongly and get upset when a friend goes | No, donkeys never notice other donkeys and do not care who they live with | No, donkeys like to be alone | Yes, but only if it is very hungry
test: «2 donkeys and a mule carrying bags, how many hooves on the path» | 12 | 8 | 3 | 16
test: Why are tens of millions of donkeys still working today? | They carry water, crops and firewood in many places | They race on special tracks | They are used to herd cattle | They pull every car in the desert
test: A donkey keeps rolling in the same sandy patch, and its friends take turns after it. What is it doing? | Cleaning its coat, scratching itches and keeping flies off | Trying to dig a hole down to find water hidden under the ground | Looking for a lost hoof | Making a bed to sleep in
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the so-called stubbornness of the donkey. | The animal halts to assess a perceived danger rather than taking flight | The animal declines to proceed owing to idleness | The animal is incapable of forward motion upon rough ground | The animal halts in order to express its hostility and resentment towards its handler
exam: Identify the parentage of the mule. | A male donkey and a female horse | A male horse and a female zebra | Two donkeys of differing colouration | A female donkey and a male goat
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the dietary requirements of the donkey? | Its diet should consist principally of straw and coarse grass, rich food being detrimental | Its diet should consist principally of baked goods | Its diet should consist exclusively of fresh fruit and vegetables, such as carrots and apples | Its diet should consist of oats and sugar in abundance
exam: Ascertain the reason for which donkeys require shelter in climates of high rainfall. | Their coats, adapted to arid conditions, are not waterproof | Their hearing is impaired by the sound of rain | Their hooves dissolve upon prolonged wetting | Their eyesight is temporarily lost in conditions of heavy and prolonged rain
